## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A smooth oriented knot $K\subset S^3$, its exterior $E_K$, tubular neighborhood $N(K)$, boundary torus $T_K$, meridian $\mu$, preferred longitude $\lambda$, and coprime integers $p,q$ not both zero.

[F1] [[thm-mayer-vietoris-sequence-in-singular-homology]]: for an open cover $X=U\cup V$, the singular-homology Mayer–Vietoris sequence is exact.

[F2] [[cor-homology-of-spheres]]: for $n\ge1$, the reduced singular homology of $S^n$ is $\mathbb Z$ in degree $n$ and zero in all other degrees; in particular $H_1(S^3;\mathbb Z)=H_2(S^3;\mathbb Z)=0$ and $H_1(S^1;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z$.

[F3] [[ex-mayer-vietoris-computation-of-the-torus-first-homology]]: $H_1(T^2;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z$, generated by the two factor circles.

[F4] [[prop-singular-homology-is-invariant-under-deformation-retracts]]: a deformation-retract inclusion induces an isomorphism on singular homology.

[F5] [[def-oriented-slope-and-knot-dehn-filling]]: the filling glues a solid torus to $E_K$ with its meridian identified with the class $p[\mu]+q[\lambda]$.

[A1] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is the stated axiom [[def-countable-choice]]. The chosen meridian bounds a disk in $N(K)$ and the degree-one preferred longitude maps to a generator of $H_1(N(K);\mathbb Z)$ [[def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude]]. These are the batch-57 peripheral definitions' hypotheses; their bodies are present and were checked. This argument assumes the exterior generator and longitude-zero conditions stated above and does not invoke the separate full-AC exterior-homology theorem.

## Proof

1.1 Choose a bicollar of $T_K$ in $S^3$ and thicken $E_K$ and $N(K)$ slightly to an open cover. The two sets deformation-retract to $E_K$ and $N(K)$, and their intersection deformation-retracts to $T_K$. By [F1] and [F2], the segment $H_2(S^3;\mathbb Z)\to H_1(T_K;\mathbb Z)\to H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)\oplus H_1(N(K);\mathbb Z)\to H_1(S^3;\mathbb Z)$ has zero groups at both ends, so the middle map is an isomorphism. [given, F1, F2, F4, construct]

1.2 Let $M=E_K(p/q)$, write $\alpha=p\mu+q\lambda$, and choose $\beta=r\mu+s\lambda$ with $ps-qr=1$, so $\alpha,\beta$ form a basis of $H_1(T_K;\mathbb Z)$. In the Mayer–Vietoris map for $M$, $\alpha$ maps to $(p[\mu],0)$ because it bounds the attached solid-torus meridian disk, and $\beta$ maps to $(r[\mu],\pm v)$ where $v$ generates the first homology of the attached solid torus; the coefficient is $\pm1$ because $\beta$ intersects $\alpha$ once. The spaces $E_K$, $T_K$, and the attached solid torus are connected, so the following $H_0$ map is injective and $H_1(M;\mathbb Z)$ is the cokernel of this map. [given, A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, construct]

2.1 By [F3], $H_1(T_K;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z^2$, and by [F4] and [F2] the solid torus $N(K)\simeq S^1$ has $H_1(N(K);\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z$. Since $H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)\oplus\mathbb Z\cong\mathbb Z^2$, the direct summand $H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)$ is free of rank one. Under the isomorphism in step 1.1, $\mu$ maps to $([\mu],0)$ because it bounds a disk in $N(K)$, while $\lambda$ maps to $(0,\pm1)$ by [A1]. Since the map is an isomorphism, $[\mu]$ is a generator of $H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)$ and $[\lambda]=0$. [step 1.1, A1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]

3.1 Thus $H_1(M;\mathbb Z)$ has generators $[\mu],v$ and relations $p[\mu]=0$ and $r[\mu]\pm v=0$. Eliminating $v$ leaves $\mathbb Z\langle[\mu]\rangle/\langle p[\mu]\rangle$. If $p\ne0$, this is $\mathbb Z/|p|\mathbb Z$; if $p=0$, coprimality gives $q=\pm1$ and the group is $\mathbb Z$. [step 2.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
